WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.914
[MUSIC]

00:00:04.914 --> 00:00:08.331
Would you prefer if this was
a interview on bikes right now?

00:00:08.331 --> 00:00:09.512
&gt;&gt; Absolutely not [LAUGH].

00:00:09.512 --> 00:00:13.810
&gt;&gt; Have you ever done a class for
600 people?

00:00:13.810 --> 00:00:14.580
&gt;&gt; Never.

00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:15.226
&gt;&gt; Would you want to try?

00:00:15.226 --> 00:00:17.268
&gt;&gt; Yes.
&gt;&gt; Okay [LAUGH]

00:00:17.268 --> 00:00:19.225
&gt;&gt; Right after this meet in the quad.

00:00:19.225 --> 00:00:22.020
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Ruth, thank you so much for being here,

00:00:22.020 --> 00:00:26.190
for coming to Stanford, for being a part
of the View From The Top program.

00:00:26.190 --> 00:00:27.730
We have a lot to cover.

00:00:27.730 --> 00:00:29.550
&gt;&gt; We do, thank you.

00:00:29.550 --> 00:00:31.060
&gt;&gt; If it's all right,
can we just jump right in?

00:00:31.060 --> 00:00:31.990
&gt;&gt; Let's do it.

00:00:31.990 --> 00:00:34.850
&gt;&gt; So I want to take us back,
all the way back.

00:00:34.850 --> 00:00:37.950
You described some very key
moments in your childhood.

00:00:37.950 --> 00:00:42.020
Being pivotal to the successes and
challenges and

00:00:42.020 --> 00:00:45.910
the obstacles you ultimately overcame
both personally and professionally.

00:00:47.090 --> 00:00:48.980
And a lot of that comes from dance.

00:00:48.980 --> 00:00:50.590
What role did dance
play in your childhood?

00:00:51.980 --> 00:00:53.290
&gt;&gt; It played a huge role.

00:00:53.290 --> 00:00:56.310
I discovered ballet classes
when I was eight years old.

00:00:56.310 --> 00:00:58.160
My mother decided it would be a good idea.

00:00:58.160 --> 00:00:59.960
She said I was getting a belly.

00:00:59.960 --> 00:01:00.904
Thanks, Mom.

00:01:00.904 --> 00:01:04.130
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And so she put me in dance class.

00:01:04.130 --> 00:01:08.850
Little did she know she basically
created a monster in that.

00:01:08.850 --> 00:01:11.500
I just took to it immediately and

00:01:11.500 --> 00:01:16.510
there was something about dancing that
just made me feel great, it was an escape.

00:01:16.510 --> 00:01:22.550
And back to my mother,
she was a difficult woman.

00:01:22.550 --> 00:01:26.170
She was narcissistic,
there wasn't much room for

00:01:26.170 --> 00:01:29.160
me, there wasn't much room for my voice.

00:01:29.160 --> 00:01:34.130
And so in retrospect, when I look back,
it made sense that I got so

00:01:34.130 --> 00:01:38.920
addicted to dancing, because it became my
way of expressing myself without words,

00:01:38.920 --> 00:01:39.800
without a voice.

00:01:41.090 --> 00:01:46.350
&gt;&gt; And I think, in your story,
there's so much grit and

00:01:46.350 --> 00:01:51.450
perseverance, that I think, explains
a lot of where you were able to get.

00:01:51.450 --> 00:01:55.980
And you described overcoming challenges
like the relationship with your mother.

00:01:55.980 --> 00:02:00.340
That often get overlooked when we talk
about the typical type of challenges here

00:02:00.340 --> 00:02:03.490
in Silicon Valley, or in leadership,
or in entrepreneurship.

00:02:03.490 --> 00:02:06.200
Could we spend a minute just
on those types of setbacks?

00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:09.380
What was it like, even in a household and

00:02:09.380 --> 00:02:11.880
in your relationship with your mother,
trying to

00:02:11.880 --> 00:02:15.210
fit into someone else's definition of
success on such a personal context.

00:02:16.770 --> 00:02:19.590
&gt;&gt; Well it had major repercussions for me,

00:02:19.590 --> 00:02:22.360
because growing up with
a mother like that.

00:02:23.480 --> 00:02:25.570
Your self esteem is not
going to be very high.

00:02:26.820 --> 00:02:30.590
And if you're always deferring to someone.

00:02:30.590 --> 00:02:34.740
And that person is always telling
you that they know more than you do.

00:02:34.740 --> 00:02:38.680
It would make sense, it would be very
hard to build any kind of confidence.

00:02:38.680 --> 00:02:43.700
So, that kind of really
spelled out my future.

00:02:43.700 --> 00:02:49.670
And I'm a huge proponent of therapy and
we might get to that later.

00:02:49.670 --> 00:02:54.630
But it was really the work that
I did in therapy that helped me

00:02:54.630 --> 00:02:58.050
the most in terms of discovering who I am?

00:02:58.050 --> 00:03:00.230
Why I am who I am?

00:03:00.230 --> 00:03:07.410
And how to build the confidence to which
had gotten me to where I have gotten to.

00:03:07.410 --> 00:03:09.580
&gt;&gt; And just staying on that point for
a minute.

00:03:09.580 --> 00:03:11.930
You described in your
memoir that years later,

00:03:11.930 --> 00:03:16.200
after launching very successful brands and
being a very successful entrepreneur in

00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:19.390
almost any dimension that you
look at entrepreneurship from.

00:03:19.390 --> 00:03:24.140
You were divorced and not yet
remarried and every winter, was it,

00:03:24.140 --> 00:03:27.915
your mother would write a card saying
here's to a better year next year.

00:03:27.915 --> 00:03:30.020
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:03:31.310 --> 00:03:33.420
&gt;&gt; And I know that seems extreme,
but when I read it,

00:03:33.420 --> 00:03:35.690
I felt that that actually
is quite relatable.

00:03:35.690 --> 00:03:38.960
There are people in all of our lives
where they demand something that

00:03:38.960 --> 00:03:41.260
we feel we have to go up hold.

00:03:41.260 --> 00:03:44.190
What was it like struggling
through that version of success?

00:03:44.190 --> 00:03:46.550
That wasn't your own.

00:03:46.550 --> 00:03:48.560
&gt;&gt; I mean, it was part generational.

00:03:48.560 --> 00:03:55.210
I would say for my mother, but actually
the words were same thing every year.

00:03:55.210 --> 00:03:58.340
Hopefully this year will be a better year.

00:03:58.340 --> 00:04:01.078
That was after I started soulcycle.

00:04:01.078 --> 00:04:05.990
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So [LAUGH] when I say generational

00:04:05.990 --> 00:04:10.510
what she meant every year when she wrote
these cards was i hope you find a man.

00:04:12.240 --> 00:04:19.440
And I find it interesting to
myself that it took me a long time

00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:25.720
after divorce to really settle settle
into a relationship with a man.

00:04:25.720 --> 00:04:30.840
And I don't know, if I subconsciously
knew that I really shouldn't

00:04:30.840 --> 00:04:36.760
go that route I really need to figure out
myself first before that falls into place.

00:04:36.760 --> 00:04:39.832
&gt;&gt; And going right there to that moment in
life where you described being divorced

00:04:39.832 --> 00:04:42.775
with two young daughters, I think they
were six years old, is that right?

00:04:42.775 --> 00:04:43.991
&gt;&gt; Six, mm-hm.

00:04:43.991 --> 00:04:46.808
&gt;&gt; What was going through your mind
as the next step, both personally and

00:04:46.808 --> 00:04:49.520
then professionally,
it seems like would follow later.

00:04:49.520 --> 00:04:52.670
&gt;&gt; Honestly, Mike, I had no idea.

00:04:52.670 --> 00:04:55.250
I was Fish out of water.

00:04:55.250 --> 00:04:58.740
I made this difficult decision
to leave the marriage.

00:04:58.740 --> 00:05:01.030
Six year old twin girls in tow.

00:05:01.030 --> 00:05:06.980
I was now a single mother didn't
have much on the financial front and

00:05:06.980 --> 00:05:09.750
had to figure out my life again.

00:05:09.750 --> 00:05:15.900
Having no idea how to even start and
that was when I found Spinning.

00:05:17.050 --> 00:05:18.310
&gt;&gt; Walk us through that because today,

00:05:18.310 --> 00:05:22.485
Spinning is, do we have any Soul Cycle
fans or Fly Will folks now.

00:05:22.485 --> 00:05:23.810
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; Wow.

00:05:23.810 --> 00:05:26.000
Thank you, that's amazing.

00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:26.840
&gt;&gt; So today at it.

00:05:26.840 --> 00:05:30.500
It is part of a lexicon,
it's in our culture and society,

00:05:30.500 --> 00:05:32.650
but what year was it when
you started Spinning?

00:05:32.650 --> 00:05:35.680
What was the landscape like because,
I imagine, it's very different than today?

00:05:35.680 --> 00:05:40.000
&gt;&gt; So, I was, again,
newly out of a marriage,

00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:43.800
embarking on this entirely
foreign new life.

00:05:43.800 --> 00:05:48.370
And when you're going through a trauma,
one thing that I learned that's really

00:05:48.370 --> 00:05:53.960
important is to take care of yourself and
stay healthy and strong.

00:05:53.960 --> 00:05:57.130
And so I had a gym membership

00:05:57.130 --> 00:06:01.070
at what was the Reebok Club on the Upper
West Side of Manhattan at the time.

00:06:01.070 --> 00:06:04.430
And it was this beautiful
full-service gym that offered

00:06:04.430 --> 00:06:08.780
every kind of group fitness you could
imagine, swimming pool, basketball court.

00:06:08.780 --> 00:06:13.480
Really incredible place and I was able

00:06:13.480 --> 00:06:18.550
to keep my gym membership with the divorce
and so I went there every day.

00:06:18.550 --> 00:06:23.270
And I used to see the spin classes going
on and I found it very intriguing.

00:06:23.270 --> 00:06:27.680
It was kind of a floor to
ceiling glass room, dark.

00:06:27.680 --> 00:06:32.310
Room was full the sound of
the wheels club music blaring.

00:06:32.310 --> 00:06:36.658
And I thought, that looks interesting but,
I was intimidated and

00:06:36.658 --> 00:06:41.300
didn't know if I could really get
myself in there by myself and try it.

00:06:41.300 --> 00:06:41.990
But I did.

00:06:41.990 --> 00:06:46.580
And literally within the first class,
I knew that this was

00:06:46.580 --> 00:06:51.440
something beyond exercise,
it felt like an experience for me.

00:06:51.440 --> 00:06:54.030
It was a cathartic experience.

00:06:54.030 --> 00:06:58.590
I felt that in the 45
minutes from beginning to end

00:06:58.590 --> 00:07:01.180
there was almost a mini-transformation.

00:07:01.180 --> 00:07:07.830
I got off the bike at the end of
the class and felt empowered, and felt

00:07:07.830 --> 00:07:13.330
that I could really conquer my day and
the challenges that presented themselves.

00:07:13.330 --> 00:07:17.620
&gt;&gt; As a quick time out to that I love
how in business school we do cases and

00:07:17.620 --> 00:07:21.240
courses and six month learning programs to
try to have a light bulb moment that you

00:07:21.240 --> 00:07:23.010
got getting on a bike one day.

00:07:23.010 --> 00:07:23.837
&gt;&gt; Yes.
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:07:23.837 --> 00:07:26.080
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Immediately.

00:07:26.080 --> 00:07:27.435
&gt;&gt; Sounds like you took the shortcut.

00:07:27.435 --> 00:07:30.480
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Thanks for that perspective.

00:07:30.480 --> 00:07:30.980
&gt;&gt; Any time.

00:07:32.290 --> 00:07:34.080
So, you get into Spinning.

00:07:34.080 --> 00:07:35.970
It sounds like you learned
a lot about yourself first.

00:07:35.970 --> 00:07:40.190
It wasn't a lightbulb moment, necessarily,
to, I need to go create a brand.

00:07:40.190 --> 00:07:43.030
What did you find out when you did some
of the soul searching that seemed to come

00:07:43.030 --> 00:07:44.885
from getting into the bike.

00:07:44.885 --> 00:07:49.282
&gt;&gt; It really helped me realize how

00:07:49.282 --> 00:07:54.500
much strength I needed to build.

00:07:54.500 --> 00:07:58.930
And again,
there was something about this that

00:07:58.930 --> 00:08:03.910
allowed me to recognize that this was
the way that I was going to do it and

00:08:03.910 --> 00:08:08.530
it It just showed me
the importance of confidence and

00:08:08.530 --> 00:08:13.300
believing in myself and
believing in my ideas.

00:08:13.300 --> 00:08:16.728
And that my mother really
didn't know everything.

00:08:18.622 --> 00:08:22.154
&gt;&gt; And that type of learning,
I think in a school like the GSP,

00:08:22.154 --> 00:08:26.466
where there's a very touchy feely
mentality to approaching business,

00:08:26.466 --> 00:08:29.452
that resonates I'm sure
with us in the audience.

00:08:29.452 --> 00:08:32.278
But can you break it down to
what that actually meant for

00:08:32.278 --> 00:08:36.211
going later into business and how you
applied some of that personal learning

00:08:36.211 --> 00:08:38.910
into actually making a business around it?

00:08:38.910 --> 00:08:45.270
&gt;&gt; Well, I really focused a lot on
my experience and how it helped me.

00:08:45.270 --> 00:08:51.040
And very quickly,
I realized everybody has trauma.

00:08:51.040 --> 00:08:53.810
Everybody has problems and
challenges in their life.

00:08:53.810 --> 00:08:57.290
I saw what it did for me, and I so

00:08:57.290 --> 00:09:02.120
believed in it that I just wanted to
share my experience with everyone else.

00:09:02.120 --> 00:09:06.970
And I wanted everyone else to have
the same chance to empower themselves.

00:09:06.970 --> 00:09:12.840
And so,
that was one of the motivating forces in

00:09:12.840 --> 00:09:18.990
my getting to where I got to with
this concept of spinning, so, yeah.

00:09:18.990 --> 00:09:22.100
&gt;&gt; Do you remember that conversation
you had where it all of a sudden

00:09:22.100 --> 00:09:25.040
started to come together with
your co-founders at the time or

00:09:25.040 --> 00:09:29.740
your would-be co-founders
in starting SoulCycle?

00:09:29.740 --> 00:09:33.590
&gt;&gt; One of the co-founder's
approached me with the idea.

00:09:33.590 --> 00:09:37.816
It was an idea I dreamed about, but
never had the capital, and she did.

00:09:37.816 --> 00:09:41.389
And so, she clearly,
she was a fan of mine,

00:09:41.389 --> 00:09:47.130
a big rider in my class, and
clearly recognized my passion for it.

00:09:47.130 --> 00:09:53.370
She didn't know anything about teaching
spin or what went into it, and so

00:09:53.370 --> 00:09:59.950
she depended on me to be the face of
the business and to provide the method.

00:09:59.950 --> 00:10:02.000
And so she definitely appreciated it.

00:10:03.180 --> 00:10:07.240
&gt;&gt; And as the discussions got going
you write and I quote, it had been

00:10:07.240 --> 00:10:11.470
a quarter century since I gave up on my
dream to become a professional dancer.

00:10:11.470 --> 00:10:16.676
This is on the brink of starting a brand
at the time, a fledging start up.

00:10:16.676 --> 00:10:18.044
&gt;&gt; Yes.
&gt;&gt; What's going through your mind

00:10:18.044 --> 00:10:19.395
the night before you start SoulCycle?

00:10:20.770 --> 00:10:21.920
&gt;&gt; The night before we opened?

00:10:21.920 --> 00:10:23.828
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:10:23.828 --> 00:10:28.204
&gt;&gt; A lot of pride and
appreciation really for

00:10:28.204 --> 00:10:31.460
everything that lead up to it.

00:10:31.460 --> 00:10:36.120
We hear this a lot, but I lived it, and
it's so true that everything we do in our

00:10:36.120 --> 00:10:42.840
life and everything that creates this path
that we take contributes to where we land.

00:10:42.840 --> 00:10:47.550
And you brought up the dance
classes before, they so

00:10:47.550 --> 00:10:52.300
tied in to the spin class that
I eventually helped to create.

00:10:52.300 --> 00:10:57.720
And when I say that, what I mean is I was
obsessed with music from an early age.

00:10:57.720 --> 00:11:00.380
And through dance classes,
I learned so much about music and

00:11:00.380 --> 00:11:02.220
different genres of music.

00:11:02.220 --> 00:11:04.976
Being a dancer, I choreograph to music.

00:11:04.976 --> 00:11:08.810
I knew how to move to music and,
really, to some extent,

00:11:08.810 --> 00:11:14.170
what I did was I just transfer
that experience on to a bike.

00:11:14.170 --> 00:11:20.220
The discipline it takes to be a dance
student and to grow up as a dancer and

00:11:20.220 --> 00:11:26.320
become a professional dancer,
that also transferred to the spin class.

00:11:26.320 --> 00:11:32.630
When I started SoulCycle, we decided that
people should applaud after the class.

00:11:32.630 --> 00:11:35.834
The instructor puts a lot into it,
especially when

00:11:35.834 --> 00:11:40.243
you're going into a boutique experience,
you're paying up for it.

00:11:40.243 --> 00:11:41.409
And you're paying for

00:11:41.409 --> 00:11:45.970
an instructor spending two hours the night
before making his or her playlist.

00:11:45.970 --> 00:11:51.070
And the applause is also for each other,
and for encouraging the community.

00:11:51.070 --> 00:11:54.880
I mean, that's just a simple thing
that I learned from dance class.

00:11:54.880 --> 00:12:00.910
And the other really key ingredient
to the success of boutique

00:12:00.910 --> 00:12:05.980
fitness is the personal connection
that we would make with our riders.

00:12:05.980 --> 00:12:08.790
And again,
no different from a dance class.

00:12:08.790 --> 00:12:10.270
If I was in a dance class,

00:12:10.270 --> 00:12:15.490
and my dance teacher said beautiful job,
Ruth, that took me through my day.

00:12:15.490 --> 00:12:20.456
That made my day, and so, in a spin class
if you point out someone in the class,

00:12:20.456 --> 00:12:25.218
if you make that personal connection,
they're coming back the next day.

00:12:27.392 --> 00:12:31.135
&gt;&gt; So you start SoulCycle and,
plot twist, it works!

00:12:31.135 --> 00:12:34.630
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; What did you learn from

00:12:34.630 --> 00:12:37.040
that bootstrapping experience?

00:12:37.040 --> 00:12:40.240
Because it wasn't that you went out,
you raised a lot of money, figured it out.

00:12:40.240 --> 00:12:41.910
In fact,
we can get to the growth part later.

00:12:41.910 --> 00:12:46.690
You were against expanding super fast and
getting off to the races.

00:12:46.690 --> 00:12:49.700
You described a moment where in
your first center, I believe,

00:12:49.700 --> 00:12:53.380
they wouldn't allow you to put signage
on the outside of the street entrance.

00:12:53.380 --> 00:12:57.380
So you made it a cool have-to-know-someone
thing to walk through a gym and

00:12:57.380 --> 00:12:58.918
get to the back near the exit-
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:12:58.918 --> 00:13:00.970
&gt;&gt; To find the SoulCycle gym.

00:13:00.970 --> 00:13:04.520
What were the learnings there from doing
it yourself and not raising capital?

00:13:04.520 --> 00:13:08.054
&gt;&gt; Well, truthfully,
that was completely luck.

00:13:08.054 --> 00:13:09.011
We signed a lease and

00:13:09.011 --> 00:13:13.365
it was after we signed the lease that
the landlord said, by the way, no signage.

00:13:13.365 --> 00:13:16.755
And we thought, my god,
what are we going to do?

00:13:16.755 --> 00:13:20.555
And, because we were in the back of the
building, you can see us from the street.

00:13:20.555 --> 00:13:23.775
And Elizabeth, one of my co-founders,

00:13:23.775 --> 00:13:29.785
came up with the idea of just putting this
rickshaw outside, painting it yellow and

00:13:29.785 --> 00:13:33.525
putting the name of our business
on it to attract attention.

00:13:33.525 --> 00:13:39.470
But, again,
it eventually became just what you said,

00:13:39.470 --> 00:13:44.140
people had to know about it
to to go there, and again,

00:13:44.140 --> 00:13:49.640
completely unintentional but
it took on this kind of cool factor.

00:13:49.640 --> 00:13:53.460
And you had to, you had to know
someone to know that it was there.

00:13:53.460 --> 00:14:00.780
And in terms of learning lesson, I mean
that one, we had no idea but it worked.

00:14:00.780 --> 00:14:04.880
And it's interesting,
I believe a lot in kind of

00:14:04.880 --> 00:14:09.630
trickle down theory in
that the aura that you

00:14:09.630 --> 00:14:14.880
create in your business has a lot to do
with who's at the top and the founders.

00:14:14.880 --> 00:14:20.078
And, eventually, the partnership didn't
work out, and the truth is my two

00:14:20.078 --> 00:14:26.180
co-founders were similar to each other,
but very different from me.

00:14:27.410 --> 00:14:32.991
And I feel that the business took
on a lot of their personality.

00:14:32.991 --> 00:14:37.536
Interestingly, when I started
Flywheel with two men,

00:14:37.536 --> 00:14:41.370
the dynamic was completely different.

00:14:41.370 --> 00:14:45.536
There was a certain respect
that the three of us had for

00:14:45.536 --> 00:14:50.536
each other, respect for
each other's areas of expertise, and

00:14:50.536 --> 00:14:54.158
we created a very
different aura at Flywheel.

00:14:54.158 --> 00:14:57.512
And to summarize it,
I would always talk about

00:14:57.512 --> 00:15:01.812
SoulCycle eventually becoming
a very exclusive feeling,

00:15:01.812 --> 00:15:07.496
having a very exclusive feeling while
Flywheel had a very inclusive feeling.

00:15:07.496 --> 00:15:12.941
We were about including everyone,
every shape, size and color.

00:15:12.941 --> 00:15:17.955
SoulCycle took on a certain,
I'm blanking on the word,

00:15:17.955 --> 00:15:22.870
but there was like a prestige
about going to SoulCycle.

00:15:22.870 --> 00:15:30.210
And, again, no judgement here, it worked,
and obviously, SoulCycle became huge.

00:15:30.210 --> 00:15:34.860
And so both personalities worked,
but they were just very different.

00:15:34.860 --> 00:15:36.800
&gt;&gt; Can you talk a little
bit more about partnership?

00:15:36.800 --> 00:15:40.830
I feel like when you decide to go
after something who you go in with is

00:15:40.830 --> 00:15:42.580
probably the most critical piece.

00:15:42.580 --> 00:15:45.100
This seemed, as you described it,
somewhat happenstance,

00:15:45.100 --> 00:15:47.410
as you were approached by
one of the co-founders.

00:15:47.410 --> 00:15:52.000
Where was the tension, ultimately,
in terms of thinking about growth,

00:15:52.000 --> 00:15:53.240
what you all wanted?

00:15:53.240 --> 00:15:54.500
What were the key learnings from that?

00:15:55.690 --> 00:15:57.370
&gt;&gt; Really important question and

00:15:57.370 --> 00:16:00.500
I get this all the time about
choosing partners in business.

00:16:00.500 --> 00:16:06.027
And the bottom line is, obviously,
there's no guarantee ever.

00:16:06.027 --> 00:16:10.850
Sometimes, I think about the fact that
My two co-founders of SoulCycle are two

00:16:10.850 --> 00:16:15.180
women who I didn't know for
a long time and I thought, well,

00:16:15.180 --> 00:16:20.260
maybe you should only go into business
with someone you do know for a long time.

00:16:20.260 --> 00:16:21.410
But that's not the case either.

00:16:21.410 --> 00:16:25.440
I actually just heard a story of two
friends who started a business and

00:16:25.440 --> 00:16:29.880
knew each other for 25 years and
sure enough, they start the business.

00:16:29.880 --> 00:16:34.640
Money gets involved,
the relationship fell apart and

00:16:34.640 --> 00:16:37.720
the business partnership dissolved.

00:16:37.720 --> 00:16:41.440
But what I learned was

00:16:43.790 --> 00:16:49.020
it really helps to know yourself when
it comes to picking partners and

00:16:49.020 --> 00:16:53.555
the truth is when I made the agreement
to go into business with these two

00:16:53.555 --> 00:17:00.280
co-founders I didn't know myself, I really
hadn't done that kind of exploration.

00:17:00.280 --> 00:17:03.200
And what I ended up doing was

00:17:03.200 --> 00:17:07.950
I ended up with two partners who
were very much like my mother.

00:17:07.950 --> 00:17:12.790
And they were two people that I,
my knee jerk was to defer to them.

00:17:12.790 --> 00:17:16.700
To really let them overpower me and
to not have a voice,

00:17:16.700 --> 00:17:20.100
I very easily slipped into that role.

00:17:20.100 --> 00:17:24.340
And as a result,
the partnership fell apart.

00:17:24.340 --> 00:17:29.060
And obviously,
that was a huge learning lesson for me,

00:17:29.060 --> 00:17:31.390
a huge light bulb moment for me.

00:17:31.390 --> 00:17:35.640
The other very obvious one was,

00:17:35.640 --> 00:17:41.130
have really good legal protection, when
you go into a partnership in a business.

00:17:41.130 --> 00:17:42.470
And I didn't.

00:17:42.470 --> 00:17:46.990
And unfortunately,
I paid a big toll for that.

00:17:49.100 --> 00:17:50.810
&gt;&gt; I appreciate the vulnerability,

00:17:50.810 --> 00:17:54.260
because that's not often a takeaway
that people would want to share, so.

00:17:55.970 --> 00:18:00.750
Going into Fly Wheel, which,
a lot of people when they hear your story,

00:18:00.750 --> 00:18:02.800
they think of SoulCycle, or
they think of Fly Wheel,

00:18:02.800 --> 00:18:05.350
they don't think that you
actually were behind both.

00:18:05.350 --> 00:18:09.200
And you described Fly Wheel as
your first true business venture.

00:18:09.200 --> 00:18:10.810
&gt;&gt; Yes.
&gt;&gt; Can you walk us through that transition

00:18:10.810 --> 00:18:11.570
of leaving Soul?

00:18:11.570 --> 00:18:13.840
I believe there was a stint
as an instructor for

00:18:13.840 --> 00:18:14.900
soul,
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:18:14.900 --> 00:18:15.670
&gt;&gt; Which must have been weird.

00:18:17.080 --> 00:18:18.980
&gt;&gt; I have to take a deep breath for
this answer.

00:18:20.650 --> 00:18:28.200
I, our Separation Agreement was signed and
I was packing up my things to leave.

00:18:28.200 --> 00:18:33.560
And one of my SoulCycle co founders looked
at me and said, where are you going?

00:18:35.630 --> 00:18:40.440
And I looked at her like she had three
heads and I said, where am I going?

00:18:40.440 --> 00:18:41.580
I'm leaving.

00:18:41.580 --> 00:18:43.310
What do you mean you're leaving?

00:18:43.310 --> 00:18:46.800
We thought you'd just be
an instructor is what she said to me.

00:18:48.360 --> 00:18:54.840
And I don't even think I answered and
I turned around and started walking out,

00:18:54.840 --> 00:18:58.340
and she kind of yelled to me as I
was walking out and she said, okay,

00:18:58.340 --> 00:19:04.025
well just promise me that whatever other
offers you get will you come back to me?

00:19:04.025 --> 00:19:09.555
Because I'll top that offer and we really
want you to be here as just an instructor.

00:19:09.555 --> 00:19:12.105
And I don't even think I turned around and
I left.

00:19:13.495 --> 00:19:19.545
And I got out there and here I was now
a single mom of two girls in high school.

00:19:21.840 --> 00:19:26.690
You know,
not in a very strong position financially

00:19:27.960 --> 00:19:31.580
and I went out there to see
what I was going to do next and

00:19:31.580 --> 00:19:35.460
the reality was it was still
very early in the industry and

00:19:35.460 --> 00:19:38.870
there were no other boutique
businesses at that point.

00:19:38.870 --> 00:19:43.140
So, it really meant that I would have to
go back to one of the big box gyms and

00:19:43.140 --> 00:19:49.910
teach classes for 40 bucks a class,
and I couldn't do it.

00:19:49.910 --> 00:19:51.140
And so I went back.

00:19:53.000 --> 00:19:58.330
I went back to her and I said,
okay, well, I got this offer.

00:19:59.340 --> 00:20:03.620
And then sure enough,
she topped the offer very generously.

00:20:04.640 --> 00:20:08.216
And I decided to do it, and

00:20:08.216 --> 00:20:15.340
it was one of the hardest decisions I
ever made, and I did it for two years.

00:20:15.340 --> 00:20:21.240
And every day I walked in to teach class,
I would have to look at both of them.

00:20:21.240 --> 00:20:25.970
And I always say I probably grew
a grey hair every single day, and

00:20:25.970 --> 00:20:31.686
it was torturous and it never got
any less torturous for two years.

00:20:31.686 --> 00:20:36.740
But what happened was is I would walk in
and then I would get on my podium and

00:20:36.740 --> 00:20:41.870
get on my bike and
there I was in front of all of my people

00:20:41.870 --> 00:20:45.720
that I brought in to this business,
all the customers.

00:20:45.720 --> 00:20:50.210
And they didn't know what happened,
and that helped a little bit.

00:20:50.210 --> 00:20:51.690
Eventually they did, obviously.

00:20:51.690 --> 00:20:55.130
But I would hit the play button,
my class would start.

00:20:55.130 --> 00:21:00.140
And all of that negativity would
just go away for 45 minutes,

00:21:00.140 --> 00:21:05.290
because I was just up there doing what I
do, which was connecting with my people.

00:21:05.290 --> 00:21:07.140
They connected with me.

00:21:07.140 --> 00:21:12.030
And we all had this cathartic
experience together, and

00:21:12.030 --> 00:21:13.150
that's what got me through it.

00:21:14.530 --> 00:21:20.150
Lastly, it was the summer of
2009 where I was teaching

00:21:20.150 --> 00:21:25.010
a SoulCycle class, and it was then that I
met my future co-founder of Fly Wheels.

00:21:25.010 --> 00:21:29.250
So If I hadn't been there,
I guess that never would've happened.

00:21:30.980 --> 00:21:36.815
&gt;&gt; And right before meeting the would-be
founder of Flywheel, was there a moment,

00:21:36.815 --> 00:21:41.000
because I think we tend to glorify
entrepreneurship as a no-brainer, must do,

00:21:41.000 --> 00:21:44.220
don't look back, was there a moment
where you did look back and

00:21:44.220 --> 00:21:47.759
you think I'm now caring for
a family solely on my shoulders?

00:21:48.790 --> 00:21:53.010
This venture didn't work out and
it's a painful next two years.

00:21:53.010 --> 00:21:56.940
Did you ever think about what life could
have been had you not taken that risk?

00:21:56.940 --> 00:21:57.870
&gt;&gt; The risk with?

00:21:57.870 --> 00:21:58.440
&gt;&gt; Soulcycle?

00:22:01.210 --> 00:22:07.220
&gt;&gt; No, because I knew that
despite the negativity and

00:22:07.220 --> 00:22:13.860
the trauma, I learned a lot, and
there were so many positive parts of it.

00:22:13.860 --> 00:22:15.586
So no, I didn't regret it at all.

00:22:15.586 --> 00:22:20.800
&gt;&gt; Going into Fly Wheel,

00:22:20.800 --> 00:22:25.180
you're now at a point where you have
pioneered spinning and spin classes,

00:22:25.180 --> 00:22:28.489
and that sense of community, and
you've built a brand around it once.

00:22:29.560 --> 00:22:33.550
What did you feel like you could do
different in a space that you defined and

00:22:33.550 --> 00:22:35.140
built already?

00:22:35.140 --> 00:22:36.684
And now what would be a competitor?

00:22:36.684 --> 00:22:41.460
&gt;&gt; Well my co-founder of Fly Wheel
made it very easy because he came up

00:22:41.460 --> 00:22:46.050
with this idea of attaching
technology to the bike and

00:22:46.050 --> 00:22:50.740
bringing this little computer
screen to the bike so that we could

00:22:50.740 --> 00:22:55.455
finally measure exactly what we were doing
on the bike, how hard we were working,

00:22:55.455 --> 00:22:59.930
what our resistance levels were,
how fast we were going.

00:22:59.930 --> 00:23:07.410
And I knew that that would change
the experience significantly and

00:23:07.410 --> 00:23:11.980
I doubted it in the beginning
because Soulcycle for me was so

00:23:11.980 --> 00:23:16.240
much about the mindful component of
the ride and I thought, I don't know if we

00:23:16.240 --> 00:23:21.790
have to focus on numbers we might
compromise the mindful component that's so

00:23:21.790 --> 00:23:25.200
important to me and they just
begged me just get on the bike and

00:23:25.200 --> 00:23:29.150
just go in there by yourself and and
work with it and see what you think.

00:23:29.150 --> 00:23:34.990
And I did and
in that period of time, 40 minutes,

00:23:34.990 --> 00:23:40.710
I knew this was going to be a game changer
because I could still do the mindful part

00:23:40.710 --> 00:23:46.300
and have the numbers and I just felt like
this was taking it to the next level.

00:23:48.440 --> 00:23:51.870
&gt;&gt; You described that your partners had
a different approach about in terms of

00:23:51.870 --> 00:23:57.520
personality, but there's also a business
nugget where they felt that you could

00:23:57.520 --> 00:24:01.520
actually grow the business by attracting
folks who otherwise wouldn't be cyclers or

00:24:01.520 --> 00:24:03.700
spin goers.

00:24:03.700 --> 00:24:06.600
What was that process like of
actually identifying a new market

00:24:06.600 --> 00:24:07.960
audience where there wasn't one already?

00:24:10.170 --> 00:24:15.026
This business is,
this type of business is honestly so

00:24:15.026 --> 00:24:20.194
word of mouth for the entire time
that I was at Soul Cycle and

00:24:20.194 --> 00:24:23.735
Flywheel, we never advertised once.

00:24:23.735 --> 00:24:28.655
So again, it always came back
to connecting with people.

00:24:28.655 --> 00:24:32.815
And I connected with the Flywheel
clientele pretty much in the same way I

00:24:32.815 --> 00:24:38.185
did with the SoulCycle clientele and it
was just about making [COUGH] excuse me.

00:24:38.185 --> 00:24:42.895
Making it personal and
make making people feel noticed.

00:24:42.895 --> 00:24:45.680
At the end of the day that's what,
that's what we all want.

00:24:47.550 --> 00:24:50.470
&gt;&gt; An interesting counter
balance to that as you described

00:24:50.470 --> 00:24:54.570
that empathy should be in moderation
when going forward in business.

00:24:54.570 --> 00:24:58.510
Which also runs counter to a lot of the
things we hear about especially today and

00:24:58.510 --> 00:24:59.990
how do you lead with empathy?

00:24:59.990 --> 00:25:01.160
Build a business around empathy.

00:25:01.160 --> 00:25:05.950
You obviously have a product and a service
that made people feel a certain way.

00:25:05.950 --> 00:25:08.260
But you also cautioned to use
it in moderation, why is that?

00:25:09.770 --> 00:25:11.535
&gt;&gt; Sometimes it can get you into trouble.

00:25:11.535 --> 00:25:17.500
[LAUGH] We, we were very big on empathy.

00:25:17.500 --> 00:25:21.890
And it helped us in terms of the culture
we built with our employees, and

00:25:21.890 --> 00:25:23.130
with our customer base.

00:25:23.130 --> 00:25:26.576
And so I'm going to talk about
the positive part first,

00:25:26.576 --> 00:25:31.251
which is again making everyone feel
important, making our maintenance

00:25:31.251 --> 00:25:35.870
person feel as important as someone
on the executive leadership team.

00:25:36.930 --> 00:25:41.459
By doing that, everybody has

00:25:41.459 --> 00:25:45.770
this desire for the business to be
successful, and that's what you want.

00:25:45.770 --> 00:25:49.760
You want everyone to be as
motivated as you are as a founder,

00:25:49.760 --> 00:25:53.390
and sure enough,
that was a great way to get that.

00:25:53.390 --> 00:25:57.610
Sometimes when you get too personal,
then, it could,

00:25:57.610 --> 00:26:02.620
you know,
the business part can be compromised and

00:26:02.620 --> 00:26:07.230
you get too much into the weeds and
I would recommend that.

00:26:07.230 --> 00:26:09.110
But especially, again,

00:26:09.110 --> 00:26:14.025
in this business because with instructors,
you're dealing with a very creative,

00:26:14.025 --> 00:26:18.780
ego-driven group, and
that can be a slippery slope sometimes.

00:26:20.340 --> 00:26:21.480
&gt;&gt; To zoom out a little bit,

00:26:21.480 --> 00:26:24.816
you look at the brands that you
successfully built and led.

00:26:24.816 --> 00:26:29.180
With SoulCycle and
Flywheel I was in the classroom last week,

00:26:29.180 --> 00:26:32.260
that had a very interesting
discussion around.

00:26:32.260 --> 00:26:36.450
The place in society for
brands that make you feel certain way.

00:26:36.450 --> 00:26:41.490
And people maybe some folks here in
the audience, at least others that I know.

00:26:41.490 --> 00:26:45.330
Have talked about SoulCycle or
fire will as, another form of therapy,

00:26:45.330 --> 00:26:47.110
it's religion, it's like my church.

00:26:48.320 --> 00:26:51.510
At what point did that start
to happen where people

00:26:51.510 --> 00:26:53.670
would share with you
that type of reaction?

00:26:53.670 --> 00:26:55.800
And is that okay for
a brand to fill that void?

00:26:57.020 --> 00:26:59.900
&gt;&gt; I think so [LAUGH].

00:26:59.900 --> 00:27:01.930
And it happened right away.

00:27:01.930 --> 00:27:08.320
And the major reason that people started

00:27:08.320 --> 00:27:14.380
to feel comfortable connecting with me
because I can only speak for myself was

00:27:14.380 --> 00:27:20.075
because of my willingness to be vulnerable
in front of people and I found that,

00:27:20.075 --> 00:27:25.465
if I was vulnerable, it made them feel
comfortable enough to be vulnerable.

00:27:25.465 --> 00:27:29.095
And it's when two people
are vulnerable that they connect.

00:27:29.095 --> 00:27:32.245
And look,
we certainly know in this day and

00:27:32.245 --> 00:27:37.685
age of living the most efficient
life we can and technology and

00:27:37.685 --> 00:27:42.330
everything going digital,
it we need to connect more than ever.

00:27:42.330 --> 00:27:46.140
I mean,
it's a human need that will never go away.

00:27:46.140 --> 00:27:50.730
And so that's why I think this business
is, this type of business is so

00:27:50.730 --> 00:27:52.085
important and here to stay.

00:27:52.085 --> 00:27:57.630
&gt;&gt; And that's a nice transition
into the industry as a whole.

00:27:57.630 --> 00:28:00.800
You got into the health and
wellness space when it was really nascent.

00:28:00.800 --> 00:28:03.610
There weren't the SoulCycles and
other brands out there.

00:28:03.610 --> 00:28:08.510
Now on every corner in any neighborhood,
especially places like Palo Alto and

00:28:08.510 --> 00:28:12.730
elsewhere, but a lot of Metro cities now
you have a million different options.

00:28:12.730 --> 00:28:15.650
Health and
wellness is an $80 billion industry.

00:28:15.650 --> 00:28:18.360
Where is there space for
innovation, if at all?

00:28:20.430 --> 00:28:26.020
&gt;&gt; So it's a good question because,
in thinking about the future of all this.

00:28:27.370 --> 00:28:31.615
We're seeing what's happening with the
industry, we're seeing how it is going.

00:28:31.615 --> 00:28:36.230
Into the digital realm and look in
spinning we're seeing about the success of

00:28:36.230 --> 00:28:40.915
Peloton and
the industry's definitely changing and

00:28:40.915 --> 00:28:45.880
with the amount of choices out
there which you just spoke about,

00:28:45.880 --> 00:28:48.820
it's very different you know
when I started SoulCycle and

00:28:48.820 --> 00:28:55.220
Flywheel those were the days of people
taking six spin classes a week so

00:28:55.220 --> 00:28:59.180
you know what a formula for
success in our business is but

00:28:59.180 --> 00:29:03.180
now, there's so
many choices I call it exercise snacking.

00:29:03.180 --> 00:29:06.330
People are, people are not coming
to spring class six days a week.

00:29:06.330 --> 00:29:09.940
They're coming twice a week and then,
they're going to Barry's twice a week.

00:29:09.940 --> 00:29:14.670
And then they might be boxing or couple
days a week on their Peloton at home.

00:29:14.670 --> 00:29:17.780
And, and
I don't have the answers actually.

00:29:17.780 --> 00:29:23.110
It's very interesting to me as to where
this will go and where this will head.

00:29:24.660 --> 00:29:29.770
Health and wellness, I think, will always
be a huge industry moving forward because

00:29:29.770 --> 00:29:32.580
of the challenges we all
have in life in general and

00:29:32.580 --> 00:29:36.950
people are finally starting to recognize
that we need to take care of ourselves and

00:29:36.950 --> 00:29:40.670
we want to live long lives and,
and kind of this is how to do it.

00:29:40.670 --> 00:29:45.630
But i'm very interested in seeing
where the fitness industry

00:29:45.630 --> 00:29:50.480
particularly goes and I do feel
like there will always be room for

00:29:50.480 --> 00:29:55.960
the group fitness part
of the fitness industry.

00:29:55.960 --> 00:30:00.960
I think people always will need this human
connection that is just spoke about.

00:30:00.960 --> 00:30:03.190
The convenience factor
will not go away and

00:30:03.190 --> 00:30:07.230
people will want to be able to have the
option of doing it in their home as well.

00:30:07.230 --> 00:30:08.349
So I think there will be both.

00:30:10.140 --> 00:30:12.840
&gt;&gt; That Peloton story just
fascinates me because.

00:30:12.840 --> 00:30:14.010
&gt;&gt; It's fascinating.

00:30:14.010 --> 00:30:17.480
&gt;&gt; Here you are taking people out of
their basement stationery bike and

00:30:17.480 --> 00:30:22.100
bringing them to their neighborhood gym
and giving them a sense of real in person

00:30:22.100 --> 00:30:26.680
community and now 15 years later you have
a company that just went public saying you

00:30:26.680 --> 00:30:31.130
know what let's bring you back into your
basement And take you away from the very

00:30:31.130 --> 00:30:36.170
thing that you describe people seeking
which is community is that here to stay?

00:30:36.170 --> 00:30:41.120
&gt;&gt; Well I'm going to give John Foley a
shout out the founder of Peloton, he used

00:30:41.120 --> 00:30:45.540
to be a big Flywheeler and SoulCycler and,
&gt;&gt; But more flywheel.

00:30:45.540 --> 00:30:46.919
&gt;&gt; Yes, of course.

00:30:46.919 --> 00:30:49.323
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Yeah.

00:30:49.323 --> 00:30:53.755
So I actually was just going to talk about
the lawsuit, but I better not [LAUGH].

00:30:55.000 --> 00:30:56.310
&gt;&gt; Come on, bring it back.

00:30:56.310 --> 00:30:59.520
Sorry I didn't mean to.

00:30:59.520 --> 00:31:00.275
&gt;&gt; Okay.
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:31:00.275 --> 00:31:01.910
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Just between us

00:31:01.910 --> 00:31:03.520
friends here in the room.

00:31:03.520 --> 00:31:07.500
&gt;&gt; This is definitely going to bite me
in the ass some way but, no, I mean,

00:31:07.500 --> 00:31:11.090
there is a lawsuit going on obviously
I won't go into the details, but

00:31:11.090 --> 00:31:16.110
there is a patent lawsuit going on
against Flywheel from Peloton and

00:31:16.110 --> 00:31:21.020
it's just very amusing to me because
John modeled the Peloton bike

00:31:21.020 --> 00:31:26.265
after the Flywheel bike so that was a big,
you know, scratch my head moment.

00:31:26.265 --> 00:31:32.270
But, but what I was going to say was I was
going to give him a compliment because he,

00:31:32.270 --> 00:31:34.620
and we're actually friends.

00:31:34.620 --> 00:31:35.150
We really are.

00:31:35.150 --> 00:31:36.504
&gt;&gt; Sounds like it.
&gt;&gt; We're on good terms.

00:31:36.504 --> 00:31:38.586
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:31:38.586 --> 00:31:40.801
&gt;&gt; Scratch my head moment.

00:31:40.801 --> 00:31:43.840
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Yeah,

00:31:43.840 --> 00:31:48.955
he at Peloton they actually
did a really good job I think

00:31:48.955 --> 00:31:53.445
of bringing the community aspect
into this at home experience.

00:31:53.445 --> 00:31:57.390
And I was actually Really impressed and
surprised by it.

00:31:57.390 --> 00:32:03.540
But, they have built a huge community at
peloton I'm sure you've all read about it.

00:32:03.540 --> 00:32:06.072
And it's part of the reason for
their success I mean,

00:32:06.072 --> 00:32:09.880
peloton writers having conventions
basically and getting.

00:32:09.880 --> 00:32:11.610
Getting together all over the world.

00:32:11.610 --> 00:32:14.920
So he's actually done
a great job of doing that.

00:32:14.920 --> 00:32:20.379
But I'm also hearing a lot in in terms
of people missing the group experience,

00:32:20.379 --> 00:32:23.522
the live experience,
and wanting it back and

00:32:23.522 --> 00:32:28.175
we were actually having this
conversation before I came out here.

00:32:28.175 --> 00:32:33.190
But another phenomenon that's taken
place is a lot of kind of the bigger

00:32:33.190 --> 00:32:37.865
businesses and corporations are now
getting into this sector and

00:32:37.865 --> 00:32:41.740
SoulCycle was acquired,
Flywheel was acquired.

00:32:41.740 --> 00:32:46.950
And the businesses have changed a lot
because they've gotten more corporate and

00:32:46.950 --> 00:32:49.720
they've gotten less personal.

00:32:49.720 --> 00:32:55.315
And I've heard a lot of complaints about
that from riders at both SoulCycle and

00:32:55.315 --> 00:33:00.569
Flywheel that they miss the way it used
to be and they miss the community and

00:33:00.569 --> 00:33:05.780
the attention that was given to
the individual because it's been lost.

00:33:05.780 --> 00:33:09.345
And so that kind of speaks to wanting
to bring it back full circle to

00:33:09.345 --> 00:33:10.410
a certain degree.

00:33:12.220 --> 00:33:17.085
&gt;&gt; To bring the conversation back full
circle, one of the things we spoke

00:33:17.085 --> 00:33:22.279
right before coming on stage about was
what lessons you would give yourself

00:33:22.279 --> 00:33:27.310
as a teenager thinking about the course
of your life as an entrepreneur,

00:33:27.310 --> 00:33:31.118
the business takeaways,
you described some of them.

00:33:31.118 --> 00:33:34.436
But also we have a lot of business
school students in the audience now and

00:33:34.436 --> 00:33:38.139
a big reason people come to programs like
View From The Top is we're about to go in

00:33:38.139 --> 00:33:39.930
a lot of different ways.

00:33:39.930 --> 00:33:42.700
And I think the power
of storytelling is that

00:33:42.700 --> 00:33:46.190
there might be a nugget you can share
that maybe tomorrow, maybe in five years,

00:33:46.190 --> 00:33:49.720
or ten years, you look back on it, and
think that was actually really helpful.

00:33:49.720 --> 00:33:53.400
That was something, I remember that
story I heard in Ruth's narrative.

00:33:53.400 --> 00:33:56.068
Is there anything that you
want to make sure you get out,

00:33:56.068 --> 00:33:58.159
if there's one thing folks can take home?

00:33:58.159 --> 00:34:03.351
&gt;&gt; Yes,
that question is often verbalized by

00:34:03.351 --> 00:34:09.699
saying what would you tell
your 16 year old self and

00:34:09.699 --> 00:34:13.607
that's how I'm looking at it.

00:34:13.607 --> 00:34:18.007
And if I could do that and
if I could go back,

00:34:18.007 --> 00:34:22.533
the message would be to
relax that we're not

00:34:22.533 --> 00:34:27.075
going to have the answers when we're 16.

00:34:27.075 --> 00:34:31.767
We might not have
the answers when we're 37,

00:34:31.767 --> 00:34:36.810
but we put so
much pressure on ourselves in thinking

00:34:36.810 --> 00:34:41.284
that we should know the answer and
we don't.

00:34:41.284 --> 00:34:44.518
And I think the comforting piece of

00:34:44.518 --> 00:34:48.979
it is that every decision
we make as we evolve and

00:34:48.979 --> 00:34:54.791
grow up actually does play a part
in where we ultimately land.

00:34:54.791 --> 00:34:58.109
And as I was talking before
about my dance classes,

00:34:58.109 --> 00:35:00.279
I thought I was going to be a dancer.

00:35:00.279 --> 00:35:03.994
That idea fell through and at the moment,

00:35:03.994 --> 00:35:09.098
when it fell through,
I felt like nothing but a failure.

00:35:09.098 --> 00:35:14.468
And I was clueless as to
what I was going to do next.

00:35:14.468 --> 00:35:18.911
And again, at that moment,
I had no idea what a huge part my

00:35:18.911 --> 00:35:23.105
experience with dance would
play in my future career.

00:35:23.105 --> 00:35:28.690
And so again, it's just everything
happens for a reason as trite as that is.

00:35:28.690 --> 00:35:30.030
But it really does, and

00:35:30.030 --> 00:35:34.300
we learn an enormous amount with
every decision we make along the way.

00:35:34.300 --> 00:35:35.920
And there really is no wrong decision.

00:35:37.790 --> 00:35:40.950
&gt;&gt; If it's okay, I'd love to end
with a personal question that

00:35:40.950 --> 00:35:44.900
I think you touch on a little bit, or at
least led me to want to ask in your book.

00:35:44.900 --> 00:35:48.138
You describe a very close
relationship with your father,

00:35:48.138 --> 00:35:50.327
who I believe passed away from cancer.

00:35:50.327 --> 00:35:50.988
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; And

00:35:50.988 --> 00:35:53.683
you describe a scene where
you were at his bedside.

00:35:53.683 --> 00:35:58.021
And he said to you that and
I quote, I'm ready to go.

00:35:58.021 --> 00:36:00.990
I've accomplished everything
that I ever set out to do and

00:36:00.990 --> 00:36:02.450
have enjoyed my life fully.

00:36:03.590 --> 00:36:06.349
And then you respond by saying that,
quote,

00:36:06.349 --> 00:36:10.269
in that moment as much as I couldn't
help feeling my own sorrow,

00:36:10.269 --> 00:36:13.409
I was also inspired by his courage and
his resolve.

00:36:13.409 --> 00:36:19.674
A lot of times death makes us think
about our own mortality in certain ways.

00:36:19.674 --> 00:36:22.207
What did that have on you as you
think about the rest of your life?

00:36:22.207 --> 00:36:27.547
&gt;&gt; Honestly, I'm looking forward
to the rest of my life and

00:36:27.547 --> 00:36:31.331
everything that's happened thus far,

00:36:31.331 --> 00:36:37.320
everything that will afford me
in terms of my experiences.

00:36:37.320 --> 00:36:41.810
But that was a moment
where I really thought

00:36:41.810 --> 00:36:46.730
I just hope one day I can say that about
myself and I already feel like I can.

00:36:48.770 --> 00:36:50.690
So that feels really good.

00:36:50.690 --> 00:36:51.240
&gt;&gt; Thank you, Ruth.

00:36:51.240 --> 00:36:52.380
Let's go to questions.

00:36:52.380 --> 00:36:53.990
I think we have a few from the audience.

00:36:55.200 --> 00:36:58.280
They were presubmitted.

00:36:58.280 --> 00:36:59.782
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:37:13.169 --> 00:37:13.757
&gt;&gt; Hi Ruth.

00:37:13.757 --> 00:37:14.749
&gt;&gt; Hi.

00:37:14.749 --> 00:37:16.862
&gt;&gt; My name's Dan Knapp,
I'm a first year MBA student.

00:37:16.862 --> 00:37:20.427
And I was wondering if you could talk
a little bit about some of the leadership

00:37:20.427 --> 00:37:24.049
principles or lessons that you took from
being an instructor on the podium and

00:37:24.049 --> 00:37:25.960
you carry it into the board room as a CEO.

00:37:27.190 --> 00:37:32.800
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I think I would answer that by
again going to back to the empathy piece.

00:37:32.800 --> 00:37:38.672
And I like to share with people that
going into business, I had absolutely no

00:37:38.672 --> 00:37:43.835
business experience, and
I was raised in a family of physicians.

00:37:43.835 --> 00:37:48.590
And surrounded by my parent's friends
who were all from the medical community.

00:37:48.590 --> 00:37:52.330
So I really didn't have a clue, and so

00:37:53.640 --> 00:37:58.520
when I look back, and
look back at my leadership style,

00:37:58.520 --> 00:38:03.040
I think, well, what was my leadership
style and where did it come from?

00:38:03.040 --> 00:38:08.100
And the truth is,
my biggest experience was being a mom.

00:38:08.100 --> 00:38:13.500
And so
I looked at how I raised my children and

00:38:13.500 --> 00:38:18.380
what my style was in raising my children,
and the truth is I

00:38:18.380 --> 00:38:23.650
used a lot of qualities that
are typically attached to women.

00:38:23.650 --> 00:38:27.470
And empathy is certainly one of them.

00:38:27.470 --> 00:38:32.560
And I learned that the simple

00:38:32.560 --> 00:38:37.730
idea of the importance
of listening to people

00:38:37.730 --> 00:38:43.390
would be very important in business as
well, and listening to my employees,

00:38:43.390 --> 00:38:47.340
and listening to what works for
them and what doesn't work for them.

00:38:47.340 --> 00:38:52.270
Listening to their despair when they
made a mistake, but then encouraging

00:38:52.270 --> 00:38:56.920
them that you can learn from your mistake
and let's see what we can do with that.

00:38:56.920 --> 00:39:02.390
Again, typical ways in which I
brought up my children and so,

00:39:02.390 --> 00:39:07.420
that was really what I drew upon the most
in terms of my leadership style.

00:39:13.260 --> 00:39:15.860
&gt;&gt; Hi, my name is Kanna Hammen and
I'm an MBA one and

00:39:15.860 --> 00:39:20.100
I have been to both SoulCycle and
Flywheel many times.

00:39:20.100 --> 00:39:22.429
So thanks for
bringing all of us that experience.

00:39:22.429 --> 00:39:28.355
You talked about how important it was
to help your clients feel noticed,

00:39:28.355 --> 00:39:33.730
and important, and
like they're a part of a community.

00:39:33.730 --> 00:39:39.090
I'm curious about other
elements that you wanted

00:39:39.090 --> 00:39:47.256
to bring into both of these brands as
you were thinking about the concepts and

00:39:47.256 --> 00:39:53.920
how you brought them to life in
the brands that you did create?

00:39:53.920 --> 00:39:59.540
&gt;&gt; It was about making people
feel comfortable in every way.

00:39:59.540 --> 00:40:04.390
Customer Service, in general, was huge and

00:40:04.390 --> 00:40:07.880
is huge in this field and
in this industry.

00:40:09.560 --> 00:40:14.683
And that has to do with not
only making people feel good,

00:40:14.683 --> 00:40:18.173
but making everything easy for them.

00:40:18.173 --> 00:40:24.845
And starting Flywheel was a huge,
sorry, let me rephrase that.

00:40:24.845 --> 00:40:29.727
Being second in an industry was a huge
advantage in many ways because I got to

00:40:29.727 --> 00:40:34.376
see what worked at SoulCycle and
continue that and what didn't work and

00:40:34.376 --> 00:40:35.554
improve upon it.

00:40:35.554 --> 00:40:40.495
So a good way to answer the question
is when we started Fly Wheel

00:40:40.495 --> 00:40:43.830
we wanted to make sign up more efficient.

00:40:43.830 --> 00:40:47.899
At SoulCycle,
literally everyone walked in,

00:40:47.899 --> 00:40:52.385
waited on line and
signed up on a list, a paper list.

00:40:52.385 --> 00:40:53.156
&gt;&gt; They still do it.

00:40:53.156 --> 00:40:55.511
&gt;&gt; And they still do, yes.

00:40:55.511 --> 00:41:00.453
Immediately at Flywheel, we thought
we're adding technology to the bike, so

00:41:00.453 --> 00:41:06.076
let's be consistent, and let's have small,
portable laptops set up in our lobby area.

00:41:06.076 --> 00:41:11.122
So people don't have to wait in line,
and people can sign up by themselves.

00:41:11.122 --> 00:41:15.314
At SoulCycle, people had to reach for
their wallet and

00:41:15.314 --> 00:41:19.793
pull out some cash for
a bottle of water, and shoe rental.

00:41:19.793 --> 00:41:25.268
We made it all inclusive in our price,
so they didn't have to pay for anything.

00:41:25.268 --> 00:41:27.227
It was all done already.

00:41:27.227 --> 00:41:31.161
They didn't have to wait in line to get
shoes, we had personalized cubby holes and

00:41:31.161 --> 00:41:34.992
we saw who's coming to class a head of
time and had their shoes waiting for them.

00:41:34.992 --> 00:41:37.968
So again customer service is huge.

00:41:41.479 --> 00:41:42.051
Hi.

00:41:42.051 --> 00:41:44.084
&gt;&gt; Hi [CROSSTALK]
&gt;&gt; I'm Jen, I'm a first year MBA,

00:41:44.084 --> 00:41:45.810
thanks so much for your time.

00:41:45.810 --> 00:41:48.449
A question I have is around
something you mentioned earlier.

00:41:48.449 --> 00:41:52.848
I think a lot of us,
an ideal state would be to go to Spain,

00:41:52.848 --> 00:41:56.900
maybe a few times a week,
Palatine at home.

00:41:56.900 --> 00:42:01.175
I think the reality is that's not
accessible just based on different income

00:42:01.175 --> 00:42:01.722
levels.

00:42:01.722 --> 00:42:06.582
So I'm curious how you think
accessibility can be incorporated

00:42:06.582 --> 00:42:11.536
into the wellness industry and
boutique fitness particularly?

00:42:11.536 --> 00:42:16.518
&gt;&gt; Great question, and
it's something I'm currently actually

00:42:16.518 --> 00:42:20.842
thinking a lot about,
because there's no question,

00:42:20.842 --> 00:42:26.296
the boutique industry is really
not very accessible to everyone,.

00:42:26.296 --> 00:42:30.167
And I think that that's
really needed right now.

00:42:30.167 --> 00:42:35.126
And I know I happened to know of
businesses that are already in

00:42:35.126 --> 00:42:40.086
the works where they're coming
up with a less expensive bike

00:42:40.086 --> 00:42:44.963
which will then allow a business
that's more accessible.

00:42:44.963 --> 00:42:49.707
A less sorry,
a less expensive at home bike as well.

00:42:49.707 --> 00:42:53.879
So I know everyone's trying to think
in that direction right now, and

00:42:53.879 --> 00:42:55.869
then I think it's so important.

00:42:57.956 --> 00:42:58.504
&gt;&gt; Hi.

00:42:58.504 --> 00:43:00.556
&gt;&gt; Hi.

00:43:00.556 --> 00:43:03.003
&gt;&gt; My name is Laura, also in MBA 1.

00:43:03.003 --> 00:43:04.264
Thank you so much for being here.

00:43:04.264 --> 00:43:07.377
My question was around
the therapy topic you mentioned.

00:43:07.377 --> 00:43:08.343
I think it's so

00:43:08.343 --> 00:43:12.889
rare to see someone as successful as
you talk about how important it was.

00:43:12.889 --> 00:43:15.901
And I'm also a big proponent of mental and
physical fitness.

00:43:15.901 --> 00:43:20.526
So I was wondering for
people who maybe haven't tried it yet, or

00:43:20.526 --> 00:43:24.896
even people who have,
how would you describe what therapy or

00:43:24.896 --> 00:43:28.513
other types of mental
fitness helped you change?

00:43:28.513 --> 00:43:31.231
Or come to realize,
you touched on it a little bit, but

00:43:31.231 --> 00:43:34.900
maybe if you could talk more in depth
about what are some tangible things to

00:43:34.900 --> 00:43:37.996
help people understand what
mental fitness could look like.

00:43:37.996 --> 00:43:41.469
&gt;&gt; I'm going to give you
two anecdotes on that.

00:43:43.916 --> 00:43:47.443
I had a very difficult marriage.

00:43:47.443 --> 00:43:52.392
So my ex-husband had
anger management issues,

00:43:52.392 --> 00:43:55.416
we'll just leave it at that.

00:43:55.416 --> 00:44:00.261
And I stayed in it for a long time,
way longer than I should have, but

00:44:00.261 --> 00:44:05.365
I can't really think of it that way
because it's who I was at that time.

00:44:05.365 --> 00:44:06.821
There was a reason why I was there.

00:44:06.821 --> 00:44:11.849
And it wasn't until therapy,
in fact, my first therapy

00:44:11.849 --> 00:44:17.820
session where I sat down and
my motivation was my father was dying and

00:44:17.820 --> 00:44:21.826
I had no idea how I was
going to go on with that.

00:44:21.826 --> 00:44:27.486
And within the first session I was asked
about to describe my marriage, and I did.

00:44:27.486 --> 00:44:31.537
And it was the first time in my
life that someone said to me,

00:44:31.537 --> 00:44:36.181
do you understand that that's
actually unacceptable behavior?

00:44:36.181 --> 00:44:39.035
And I think I just broke down in tears.

00:44:42.055 --> 00:44:44.405
That was a life changing moment for
me, and

00:44:44.405 --> 00:44:47.940
that wouldn't have happened if
I didn't seek out a therapist.

00:44:47.940 --> 00:44:55.134
And that really started my ball rolling in
terms of not only leaving the marriage but

00:44:55.134 --> 00:44:59.680
starting to really learn
about who I am and who I was.

00:44:59.680 --> 00:45:04.419
The second anecdote was another trauma,
it was about another

00:45:04.419 --> 00:45:09.354
trauma which was the dissolution
of my SoulCycle partnership.

00:45:09.354 --> 00:45:13.394
And I remember probably a year ago,

00:45:13.394 --> 00:45:18.657
I mean the dissolution happened in 2007.

00:45:18.657 --> 00:45:23.939
It's 2019, and probably a year ago
I sat in a therapy session and

00:45:23.939 --> 00:45:25.760
I said to my therapist,

00:45:25.760 --> 00:45:31.789
is there something wrong with me because
I can't seem to get past this trauma.

00:45:31.789 --> 00:45:37.928
Like I still get really upset and
angry when I think about it.

00:45:37.928 --> 00:45:40.234
Is there something wrong with me?

00:45:40.234 --> 00:45:41.709
And she looked at me and

00:45:41.709 --> 00:45:46.376
she said, why would you ever think
there was something wrong with you?

00:45:46.376 --> 00:45:49.428
Why wouldn't you feel that anger today?

00:45:49.428 --> 00:45:54.128
It was a horrible, horrible thing.

00:45:54.128 --> 00:45:57.881
That was incredibly helpful to me.

00:45:57.881 --> 00:46:01.775
Again, just another moment
of putting things, and

00:46:01.775 --> 00:46:06.475
being able to put things in
perspective and have self empathy.

00:46:06.475 --> 00:46:12.640
A lot of us don't really know that we have
permission to really feel for ourselves.

00:46:12.640 --> 00:46:14.447
We've all been through traumas.

00:46:14.447 --> 00:46:19.525
We've all had a lot of challenges
growing up, and in our adult life, and

00:46:19.525 --> 00:46:24.536
taking a moment and understanding tat and
acknowledging that is huge.

00:46:27.929 --> 00:46:31.367
&gt;&gt; So we have one last thing to do
which is somewhat of a tradition here

00:46:31.367 --> 00:46:33.516
called the Lightning Round.

00:46:33.516 --> 00:46:34.359
&gt;&gt; Yep, you should be scared.

00:46:34.359 --> 00:46:35.224
&gt;&gt; That's a lot of pressure.

00:46:35.224 --> 00:46:36.406
I am.

00:46:36.406 --> 00:46:40.889
&gt;&gt; But I took the liberty to make
a Version two of that game just because

00:46:40.889 --> 00:46:43.947
you're here and
I thought it might been fun.

00:46:43.947 --> 00:46:45.719
We can see how this works.

00:46:45.719 --> 00:46:49.033
It's called who said it best,
Plato or SoulCycle?

00:46:49.033 --> 00:46:52.770
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; It's a very simple game.

00:46:52.770 --> 00:46:58.140
So I will say a quote and
you will have the option of saying

00:46:58.140 --> 00:47:03.180
is it a quote said in a Soul Cycle class,
or by Plato.

00:47:03.180 --> 00:47:06.302
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And then if you want,

00:47:06.302 --> 00:47:10.331
we can then ask the audience to either
kind of ask the audience a question.

00:47:10.331 --> 00:47:13.574
They can either affirm or
deny if that might be the right answer.

00:47:13.574 --> 00:47:15.681
And then I'l share who said it.

00:47:15.681 --> 00:47:18.289
&gt;&gt; Wait,
I have to say one thing before you start.

00:47:18.289 --> 00:47:21.206
&gt;&gt; Who's [LAUGH] I asked the same question
I was looking up something before you

00:47:21.206 --> 00:47:22.444
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] No, because that,

00:47:22.444 --> 00:47:24.199
that question brought this to mind.

00:47:24.199 --> 00:47:28.163
Can I see a raise of
hands of anyone who saw

00:47:28.163 --> 00:47:32.485
the Saturday Night Live SoulCycle parody?

00:47:32.485 --> 00:47:34.746
Okay, thank you.

00:47:34.746 --> 00:47:39.270
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; This was an inspiration for the game.

00:47:39.270 --> 00:47:41.942
&gt;&gt; It was so spot on, by the way.

00:47:41.942 --> 00:47:43.122
&gt;&gt; Okay, question number one.

00:47:43.122 --> 00:47:47.072
Everyone listen up here,
this is important.

